WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:06.401
Nia DaCosta's reimagining of 1992's "Candyman"&nbsp;
is a faithful modernization of the cult horror&nbsp;classic. 

00:00:06.401 --> 00:00:08.880
Co-written with Jordan Peele,&nbsp;
the film is a fitting tribute to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:12.560
original -- especially by the time it reaches&nbsp;
its end. We've got everything you need to fully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:14.534
understand the "Candyman" conclusion.

00:00:14.534 --> 00:00:17.608
"Shh. Don't. 

00:00:18.923 --> 00:00:19.680
Don't say that."

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:23.280
As the urban legend of Candyman goes, if&nbsp;
you say his name five times in a mirror,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:28.320
he will appear ... to kill you. In 1992, this&nbsp;
seemingly silly legend set the stage for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:33.120
complex film about race and local mythology in&nbsp;
the Cabrini-Green housing project in Chicago.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:36.480
Years later, in real life and in the&nbsp;
"Candyman" universe, the housing project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:42.000
has been demolished and replaced by luxury condos.
This is how 2021's "Candyman" begins: as a movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:47.231
about gentrification. Anthony and Brianna are a&nbsp;
Black couple who move into a newly rehabilitated building. 

00:00:47.231 --> 00:00:51.040
Anthony is a painter in an artistic&nbsp;
rut -- until he finds inspiration in the legend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:55.120
of Candyman. He learns about the community&nbsp;
folklore from William Burke, a local who was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:59.280
a child during the events of the original film.&nbsp;
Burke tells Anthony the story of police murdering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:03.840
a Black resident whom they believed to be giving&nbsp;
candy with razor blades inside to local children.&nbsp;

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:07.680
As Anthony starts to create art inspired&nbsp;
by the legend of Candyman and the history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:12.080
of racial violence at Cabrini-Green, prominent&nbsp;
figures in the Chicago art world begin getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:16.800
brutally murdered. The tension ratchets up after&nbsp;
a seemingly innocuous bee sting leads Anthony's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:21.680
hand to start decomposing into a burnt, fleshy&nbsp;
monstrosity. He grows increasingly reclusive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:26.320
and distance gathers between him and Brianna.
Slowly, Anthony begins to see his reflection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:30.000
for what it really is, and realizes&nbsp;
that he is becoming the Candyman.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:35.200
At one point in the final act, Anthony&nbsp;
visits his mother. In another smart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:38.640
casting decision, the movie brings&nbsp;
back Vanessa Williams as Anne-Marie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:43.360
creating a greater sense of continuity with the&nbsp;
original. As Burke explains earlier in the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:46.960
during the events of the original movie a&nbsp;
woman's child was kidnapped by the Candyman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:52.080
and rescued by Helen Lyle who lost her life&nbsp;
in the process. Any fan of the 1992 cult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:57.120
classic will recognize Williams as that child's&nbsp;
mother: The child, then, was Anthony himself.&nbsp;

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:00.880
Anne-Marie then explains to Anthony that&nbsp;
when he returned she knew — somehow — that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:04.960
he was destined to be the next Candyman.&nbsp;
In an attempt to have him grow up normally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:09.440
she sheltered him from all mentions of the legend.&nbsp;
But it was all for nothing: His transformation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:13.360
begins immediately after he attempts to summon&nbsp;
the murderous figure for the first time.&nbsp;

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:17.520
It's not explained extremely well, but it seems&nbsp;
that saying the phrase five times in the mirror&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:21.520
acts not just as a way for the average person&nbsp;
to summon Candyman, but as an activation for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:23.034
Anthony to become him.

00:02:24.134 --> 00:02:25.675
"Be my victim."&nbsp;

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:30.240
Whatever happened when he was kidnapped passed on&nbsp;
the Candyman gene — though that's technically not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:34.240
how the lore works. In the past, a violent&nbsp;
killing of a Black man would often lead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:38.960
to a slew of Candyman murders. In Anne-Marie's&nbsp;
explanation, it's unclear if there are therefore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:43.457
multiple Candymen with separate origins, or&nbsp;
if they all fuse into one supernatural entity.

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:48.720
William Burke is the most mysterious character&nbsp;
in "Candyman," and it can be hard to determine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:52.880
his motivations. So let's back up quickly&nbsp;
and recap Burke's importance in the movie.&nbsp;

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:56.400
Prior to the climax, the last we see of&nbsp;
Anthony is him returning to the abandoned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:00.480
neighborhood where he first discovered the&nbsp;
art depicting Candyman. When Brianna goes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:04.080
the laundromat to ask Burke where he might be,&nbsp;
she is knocked unconscious and brought to an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:08.480
empty church where she finds Anthony's entire&nbsp;
body nearly succumbed to rot. Brianna escapes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:12.400
Burke's nightmare church to a house that&nbsp;
looks eerily familiar: Eagle-eyed viewers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:16.592
will recognize it from earlier in the film as the&nbsp;
first place Anthony sees any mention of Candyman.&nbsp;

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:20.320
Burke's history, which is inextricably&nbsp;
linked to Anthony's discovery of and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:24.800
evolution into Candyman, is explained through&nbsp;
flashbacks that occur sporadically. As a kid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:29.443
he watched Sherman Fields, a local man who&nbsp;
had a hook for a hand, get murdered by police.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.443 --> 00:03:33.440
He later saw Fields come back as Candyman:&nbsp;
This flashback confirms Burke's genuine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:37.680
belief in the legend. Burke also knew that&nbsp;
Anthony was the baby Candyman had kidnapped,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:40.800
and he waited patiently for decades to&nbsp;
help him complete his transformation.

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:45.920
In the final moments of the film, several&nbsp;
white police officers invade the house and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:50.800
arrest Brianna after she shoots and kills Burke.&nbsp;
Anthony's corpse lies next to his as Brianna is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:54.800
forcefully escorted to the back of a cop car.&nbsp;
She is threatened by the cop in the driver's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:58.859
seat and promises to tell him everything&nbsp;
if he can grant her one strange request:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.859 --> 00:04:04.420
She wants to see herself in the mirror.
Brianna then summons Candyman, which seems like an&nbsp;insane decision. 

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:08.320
But if we follow the logic that&nbsp;
Anthony is the new Candyman, it helps explain why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:12.800
she calls upon a murderous ghost in a moment of&nbsp;
crisis: She's actually summoning her boyfriend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:17.161
Anthony, for help. But there is also one&nbsp;
clear thematic reason behind her choice.&nbsp;

00:04:17.161 --> 00:04:20.960
While Candyman has a history in the series of&nbsp;
slaying people indiscriminately, that's starting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:25.440
to change in the 2021 movie. In the evolving&nbsp;
'Candyman' universe, the symbolic figure is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:29.712
beginning to become a vengeful answer to white&nbsp;
violence, and a protector of the Black community.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.712 --> 00:04:34.000
The framing of the legend as the hero, instead of&nbsp;
the monster, adds a powerful layer of commentary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:38.080
to the final scene of Anthony, as Candyman,&nbsp;
brutally executing a group of white cops.&nbsp;

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:39.680
"So he's real?"&nbsp;

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:45.200
"Candyman ain't a he.&nbsp;
Candyman's the whole damn hive."

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:50.462
The abrupt and cryptic ending to "Candyman" comes&nbsp;
with a final twist that might shed light on how&nbsp;the myth works. &nbsp;

00:04:50.462 --> 00:04:54.560
During his final rampage, we see&nbsp;
Anthony finally embracing his role as Candyman. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:59.440
his face becomes slowly engulfed by bees, Anthony&nbsp;
kills the final police officer. The bees start to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:04.468
dissipate as he walks toward Brianna, revealing&nbsp;
that Anthony has been absorbed and consumed by&nbsp;Candyman. 

00:05:04.468 --> 00:05:10.587
The final shot of the movie is of actor&nbsp;
Tony Todd's face, which has replaced Anthony's,&nbsp;as he growls a closing line.

00:05:10.779 --> 00:05:12.592
"Tell… everyone."

00:05:13.833 --> 00:05:16.409
&nbsp;"New to the neighborhood! Ha ha."

00:05:16.952 --> 00:05:18.880
 
The final line is a confident mission statement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:24.240
one that is more meta-textual than anything.&nbsp;
"Candyman" is back and (possibly) here to stay.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:28.560
But this scene also marks the fulfillment of&nbsp;
Anthony's destiny to be one with Candyman.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:32.800
As he's consumed by the bees he becomes, like&nbsp;
Sherman Fields and every other Candyman before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:36.640
him, more than just a man. He becomes&nbsp;
part of the symbol that is Candyman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:42.067
represented by Daniel Robitaille -- the original&nbsp;
Candyman who, lore says, arose in the 1800s.&nbsp;

00:05:42.067 --> 00:05:46.320
The final shot of Todd is a great homage to the&nbsp;
original, but is it a final homage? It leaves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:50.800
us wondering where the franchise can go from here,&nbsp;
and whether that emphatic but cryptic proclamation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:55.262
at the very end is Todd passing the torch or&nbsp;
saying he is here to stay for another trilogy.
 &nbsp;

00:05:57.040 --> 00:06:00.240
Not to crush your hopes for a "Candyman"&nbsp;
cinematic universe, but there are no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:04.880
current plans for a sequel to the 2021 film.&nbsp;
However, the ending of "Candyman" does feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:08.720
an awful lot like a set-up for another movie.
The abruptness of the ending shot plays much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:12.400
better as a teaser for a sequel than a wrap&nbsp;
on the events of this movie. And looking at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:16.720
the whole movie symbolically, things begin to&nbsp;
click into place. It acts as a passing of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:21.600
torch from Robitaille to Anthony. Todd is in his&nbsp;
60s now, so it makes sense to use this movie as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:25.680
ceremonial transfer of the Candyman title.
Hopefully, this means we get a sequel to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:30.160
this new "Candyman" with Yahya Adbul-Mateen&nbsp;
wielding that hook to bloody effect. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:34.720
it is always a treat to see Tony Todd -- even in&nbsp;
those terrible '90s sequels -- but if "Candyman"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:39.280
wants to thrive as a franchise, it may be time to&nbsp;
let go of the past and pivot to a new Candyman.&nbsp;

00:06:40.448 --> 00:06:43.623
"Say. His. Name. Candyman."

